# simpl_ia integration guide

> **Maintained by**: the `simpl_ia` repo, auto-synced to simpl_knowledge.
> **Source of truth**: `simpl-techs/simpl_ia/.agent/SKILL.md`

## What this library is

`simpl_ia` is the shared Python library for vector search, embedding providers, document chunking, vector-store adapters (Milvus, Qdrant), and optional structured LLM clients used across the simpl platform.

Request accounting uses the coordinated tracker migration and library release.
`simpl_ia` captures each model response; `simpl_core` binds agent/node/pipeline
and business attribution. `track_cost` suppresses duplicate run aggregates when
request evidence exists. Unknown prices remain visible; never synthesize costs
from an unmatched provider-export difference.

## Installation

```bash
poetry add "simpl-ia @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_ia.git@main"
```

Install extras only when needed:

```bash
poetry add "simpl-ia[embedding-vector] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_ia.git@main"
poetry add "simpl-ia[ml] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_ia.git@main"
poetry add "simpl-ia[agents] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_ia.git@main"
poetry add "simpl-ia[chunking-chonkie] @ git+https://github.com/simpl-techs/simpl_ia.git@main"
```

Pin to a tagged release when coordinating cross-repo bumps — platform repos typically track `@main`.

## Basic usage

Remote embedding + vector search (no local torch):

```python
from simpl_ia import EmbeddingService, SearchConfig, VectorSearch
from simpl_ia.core.providers.registry import ProviderRegistry

registry = ProviderRegistry.from_entry_points()
provider = registry.get("openai-embeddings")
embedder = EmbeddingService(provider=provider)

search = VectorSearch(
    embedder=embedder,
    config=SearchConfig(collection_name="documents", top_k=10),
)
results = search.query("quarterly revenue trends")
```

Structured LLM calls (requires `[agents]` extra):

```python
import os

from simpl_ia.agents import (
    ModelPolicy,
    ModelTarget,
    ProviderBinding,
    ProviderCredentials,
    StructuredLLMClient,
    bind_policy_credentials,
)

policy = ModelPolicy(
    name="gpt-5.1",
    provider="openai",
    reasoning="high",
    fallbacks=[ModelTarget(name="gpt-5-mini", provider="openai")],
)
bindings = [
    ProviderBinding(
        provider="openai",
        credentials=ProviderCredentials(api_key=os.environ["OPENAI_API_KEY"]),
    )
]
client = StructuredLLMClient.from_policy(bind_policy_credentials(policy, bindings))
agent = client.make_agent(output_type=MySchema, system_prompt="...")
result = await client.run(agent=agent, user_prompt="...")
```

Providers: `openai`, `deepseek`, `openrouter` (models are `vendor/model` slugs),
`cheaper_inference` (Cheaper Inference's gateway; bare model ids such as
`glm-5.3-flash`, a slug is refused; the endpoint defaults to
`https://api.cheaperinference.com/v1`, so a binding needs only the key; every request
body carries `"ranking": "balance"`, the gateway's seller ranking, and no
`min_discount_percent`),
`self_hosted` (an OpenAI-compatible server you run, such as llama.cpp or vLLM:
the target must name its `base_url`, needs no key, structured output is native,
and no cost receipt is written, since no provider bills it),
and `typesafe` (an alias of `openrouter` with `api="decisions"`, below). A
target's `api` is the API its model speaks there: `chat` (the default) or
`decisions` for a decision model on `openrouter` (below).
`client.selected_target_metadata(result)`
reports which chain member answered (`model`, `provider`, `api`, `reasoning`,
`fallback_index`) and the name it answered under (`served_model`); `api` tells a
decision model's answer from a language model's. For a target naming a canonical model
(below), `model` and `provider` are the member that answered (the provider's own name)
and `canonical` the model's name; `canonical` is `None` otherwise. For a target naming a
bucket (below), `canonical` is the tier's model that answered, and `bucket` and `tier` (from
0) name the bucket and the tier; both are `None` otherwise. With an
`attempt_log` passed to `make_agent`, the member is credited by chain position,
so a provider renaming what it serves is logged instead of failing the call.

A target's `extra_body` is merged into every request body it sends, for fields the
typed settings lack (`chat_template_kwargs`). A call adds its own with
`make_agent(..., extra_body=...)`: nested objects merge and the call wins. A decision
model takes none. Every model in the chain sends the call's `extra_body`, so every one
must be `self_hosted`: `make_agent` raises `ValueError` naming each one that is not.
`make_agent(system_prompt=None)` sends no system message, for a
model whose prompt format has no place for one; `run(..., max_tokens=...)` caps the
answer of every model in the chain (a target with its own `max_output_tokens` is sent
the smaller of the two).

```python
policy = ModelPolicy(
    name="nuextract3",
    provider="self_hosted",
    base_url="http://127.0.0.1:8092/v1",
    extra_body={"chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": False}},
)
client = StructuredLLMClient.from_policy(policy)  # no binding needed
agent = client.make_agent(
    output_type=StructuredDict(answer_schema),
    system_prompt=None,
    extra_body={"chat_template_kwargs": {"template": template, "instructions": text}},
)
result = await client.run(agent=agent, user_prompt=document, temperature=0.0, max_tokens=1024)
```

An `openai` target's `prompt_cache="explicit"` caches the system prompt and nothing
after it, through pydantic-ai's `cache={"messages": False}`. On gpt-5.6 and later the
request runs in OpenAI's explicit cache mode with one breakpoint at the end of the system
prompt, so the rest is billed as uncached input instead of being written to the cache; on
an older OpenAI model it changes nothing. It is refused on any other provider, and on a
policy covers the primary only. `None` (the default) leaves OpenAI's implicit caching.

Structured output mode (native JSON schema, a forced output tool, or the
schema in the prompt) is resolved per chain member from the route's
capabilities, whether the agent passed `tools` to `make_agent`, and the
target's `output_mode` (`auto` by default; `native` / `tool` / `prompted` to
pin one, refused when the target cannot serve it). Under `auto`: OpenAI's own
endpoint is native either way (a request with function tools -- the agent's,
or an output tool such as a `ToolOutput` or `[str, Model]` spec brings -- reaches
it through the Responses API with `store` off, since Chat Completions refuses
tools while a GPT-6 model reasons); OpenRouter is native for a tool-less agent and
**tool** for an agent with tools (`require_parameters` stays a routing filter
and never puts `response_format: json_schema` next to `tools`, which the
upstreams behind that route answer without ever calling a tool); `deepseek` is
always prompted; `cheaper_inference` is always **tool** (the gateway answers a
`response_format` with a 503 on some upstreams, so `native` is refused there and
its prompted mode sends the schema in the prompt only). The resolved mode is on every attempt-log entry and on
`selected_target_metadata(result).output_mode`;
`client.resolved_output_modes(tools_present=..., output_type=...)` reports it per
target without a request, for policy tests. An output type answered through an output
tool on every model (a `ToolOutput`, or `[str, Model]`) makes every member `tool`,
whatever its route would pick.

Build every agent that runs on a policy through `make_agent`, including a plain-text
one (`output_type=str`): constructing `Agent(...)` by hand reads the model some other
way than the policy does. Every chain falls back on errors and on a response cut off
at its output limit before it answered (a thinking model that spent its whole budget
reasoning); a cut-off on the last member raises `FallbackExceptionGroup`. It falls back
too on any answer cut off at its target's own output cap (below). A text-only
agent's chain falls back on nothing else; any spec with a schema to fail (a model,
`list[Model]`, a union, a `ToolOutput`, `[str, Model]`) keeps the invalid-output guard,
which validates answers exactly as pydantic-ai does for that spec, with the agent's
`validation_context`, whenever a member answers in prompted or tool mode.

**Each model in the chain has one attempt budget per agent run**: the target's
`max_invalid_responses`, else the policy's, else `retries + 1` (**4** with
`make_agent`'s default `retries=3`: one attempt and three corrective retries). Every failed attempt on the
model spends it, across every request of the run (tool turns and corrective retries
included): an error or timeout of a sent request, a rejected answer (cut off, or
invalid with no retry left), and an invalid answer handed back for a corrective retry.
A pinned upstream (one an internal, canonical or bucket target sends to, or a closed
list split by the timeout skip) that failed in the run is not sent again in it, so the
next turn starts where the last one answered. A target the gateway routes itself (pinned
to no upstream) is never marked failed: every request of the run walks the chain from
its first target again, as long as the target's budget lasts. **Each model also has a
time share per run** of `TIME_SHARE_DEADLINES` (**2**) × its attempt deadline (below):
the wall-clock time of its failed attempts (errors, timeouts, rejected answers and
corrective retries; never an answer it kept), each counted up to one deadline, plus one
more deadline must fit in the share before another attempt is sent. A first attempt that
times out leaves room for a second; once more than one deadline is wasted the model
refuses, so it never wastes more than two deadlines; fast failures still spend the whole
attempt budget. A model with no attempt deadline has no time share. Once a model's
budget or time share is spent, or every pinned upstream it may use failed, its requests
are refused without being sent
(`simpl_ia.agents.attempt_budget.AttemptBudgetSpent`, a `ModelAPIError`, whose message
says which: `attempt budget of N spent`, `time share of S s spent`, `every upstream
failed in this run`) and the chain moves on; the last model refuses too, and the call
then fails as when its budget is spent. Nothing is spent by a timeout-skip refusal or an
HTTP 404 (a 404 still keeps a pinned upstream out of the run). An invalid answer is
handed back for a corrective retry only when the retry still fits the time share, and is
also rejected once `make_agent(retries=...)` corrective retries were made in the run,
since pydantic-ai would raise instead of asking again.
**What reaches the caller is what pydantic-ai raised before**: when the last model, or
a model alone, answers invalidly with no retry left, the call raises
`OutputBudgetSpent`, an `UnexpectedModelBehavior` with pydantic-ai's message
(`Exceeded maximum output retries (N)`), never wrapped in a `FallbackExceptionGroup`;
when the budget runs out on errors or timeouts, the call raises those errors (a single
one, or the chain's `FallbackExceptionGroup`). Each `agent.run` starts with every
budget whole, so `run`'s truncate-and-retry and temperature retry are sent normally.

**Each target has its own attempt deadline and output cap.** `attempt_timeout_seconds`
on a target is the wall-clock deadline of each attempt on it (a request, or a stream's
open), else the policy's (the primary's), else `make_agent(attempt_timeout_seconds=)`;
with none of the three the target's attempts are unbounded. `max_output_tokens` on a
target, else the policy's, is sent as `max_tokens` on that target's requests only; there
is no default cap. When the run also passes `max_tokens`, the smaller is sent. Every
upstream of an internal target shares the target's values. An answer of a capped target
that ends on `finish_reason="length"` is a failed attempt whatever it holds: its trail
entry is `rejected` with `error_class` `InvalidOutput` and `error_message` `output cut
off at its cap of N tokens (finish_reason=length)`, it spends the target's budget and
the chain moves on (next upstream, next target). On the last model, or a model alone, it
raises `simpl_ia.agents.output_cap.OutputTruncated`, an `UnexpectedModelBehavior`. A
target with no cap of its own is judged as before: a cut-off answer with text in it is
taken as an answer. Streams the caller reads are sent the cap but never judged (the
chain judges no such stream); a request the client streams internally (below) is judged
as a request. A caller bounding the whole call takes its ceiling from
`simpl_ia.agents.structured.chain_call_ceiling_seconds(targets,
attempt_timeout_seconds=<the agent's>, retries=3)`: 1.1 × the sum over targets of
min(budget, 2) × deadline, each target's time share, which no target can overrun, so
every target after it keeps its turn (264 s for one target at 120 s, 528 s for two,
1,056 s for four).

**Each stream is watched before it is handed over; a request can be hedged.** All three
settings sit on a target, else the policy (the primary); none has a default that changes
a call on its own except the statistics-derived first-token deadline on streams.

- `ttft_deadline_seconds`: how long a stream may go without its first chunk, from the
  request (the open included); unset, twice the offering's p95 time to first chunk for the
  agent (`OfferingStats.ttft_p95_ms`, below), and at least
  `FIRST_TOKEN_DEADLINE_FLOOR_SECONDS` (**20 s**), when the source measured one
  (`structured.first_token_deadline_for`), else none. A value you set is used as written,
  below the floor too. A stream with no first chunk by then
  ends with `simpl_ia.agents.watchdog.FirstTokenTimeout` (a `ModelAttemptTimeout`): its
  trail entry is `raised`, `error_class` `FirstTokenTimeout`, `timed_out: true`,
  `health_class` `timeout`; the timeout skip counts it; the chain moves on.
- `max_reasoning_tokens`: the reasoning a stream may produce before its answer starts
  (thinking characters / 4, or the reasoning tokens the usage reports, whichever is
  larger). Past it the stream is aborted with
  `simpl_ia.agents.output_cap.ReasoningTruncated` (an `OutputTruncated`): its entry is
  `rejected`, `error_class` `InvalidOutput`, `error_message` `reasoning passed its budget
  of N tokens before any answer (aborted)`, `health_class` `invalid`; the chain moves on,
  and on the last model, or a model alone, the caller gets it as an `OutputTruncated`.
  While a stream reasons within budget its events are held back and handed over, in
  order, once the answer starts. Reasoning after that is not counted.
- Either one set (on the target or the policy) also makes the client **stream the
  target's requests internally** (`structured.streams_requests`): each `run` request is
  sent as a stream and the response built from it, so the watch applies; its entries are
  `streamed`, with `latency_ms` the time to first chunk. Its attempt deadline still
  bounds it whole, as it bounds a request.
- `hedge: true` (off by default): for an agent with no tools, a request whose first
  upstream has no first chunk by its offering's p95 time to first chunk for the agent also
  goes to the next upstream of the same target, in order, whose offering is marked
  `cancel_stops_billing: true` (the first must be marked too, and its p95 measured; no
  statistics, no hedge). Both are streamed; the first to produce a chunk is read to its
  end and judged, the other is cancelled. Both entries carry `hedge`: `won` for the kept
  attempt, `lost` for the other (`outcome: cancelled`, or `raised` when it failed). Each
  spends one attempt of the target's budget (the kept one unless judging it spent one);
  a hedge is sent only when the budget has room for both. When the kept attempt fails or
  its answer is rejected, the request goes on to the target's next upstreams as usual. At
  most one hedge per agent run and target. Only internal, canonical and bucket targets
  ranked from an offering source hedge.

Pass `make_agent(..., route_log=[])` to have one JSON-serialisable entry appended per
chain target when the chain is built: `fallback_index`, `provider`, `api`, `model`,
`canonical` (the canonical model a target names, `null` for a target naming a provider),
`routing` (`internal` or `gateway`), `budget`, `attempt_timeout_seconds` and
`max_output_tokens` (as resolved for the target, `null` when unbounded or uncapped),
`token_mix` (`{input_tokens,
output_tokens, samples}` or `null`), `ranking` (an internal target's mode, `price` or
`effective`; `null` on any other target), `exploration` (`{explored, propensity, chosen}`
when the effective order was sent without an epoch, else `null`), `family` (the call's
prompt family key, `null` without an agent name), `epoch` (`{start, upstream, decision}`
when the family's epoch ordered the target, below, else `null`), `canary` (`{trial,
chosen, propensity, control}` while a canary runs for the agent on the target's model, below, else
`null`), `candidates` in the
order they are tried (`provider`, `model` (the provider's own name), `upstream`, 1-based
`rank` in the price order, `est_cost_usd`, `price_source`
`book`/`listing`, `quantization`, `zdr`, `status`, and from the effective ranking
`effective_rank` (`null` when a constraint left the upstream out), `cost_per_answer_usd`,
`latency_s`, `p_bad`, then `deadline_s`, the attempt deadline the upstream is sent with)
and `excluded` (`provider`, `model`, `upstream`, `reason`: `zdr`, `quantization`,
`not_listed`, `ignored`,
`missing_parameter:<name>`, `price_above_max`, then `health_open` (canonical and bucket
targets, below), then `precision_excluded:<precision>` (below), then
`not_capable:<capability>` and `not_certified:<capability>` (below), then `blamed` (an
upstream tried last because the agent is blamed for its failures there, always
`shadow: true`, below), then the constraint codes below; an
upstream left out by a precision policy, a capability, open health or a constraint that
is still sent carries `shadow: true`). A target sent
through one unpinned member (a plain provider, a gateway target without a closed list)
has neither; a closed list taken as written lists its upstreams in that order with `null`
for everything it does not know but `deadline_s`. Every entry also carries `bucket` and
`tier` (the bucket and the tier's index, from 0, on a bucket's tier; `null` otherwise) and
`models` (`null` but on a bucket's tier). A bucket tier's entry has `provider`, `model`,
`canonical`, `token_mix`, `attempt_timeout_seconds` and `max_output_tokens` `null`, its
`budget` is the sum of its models' budgets, and `models` lists each model of the tier
(`canonical`, `budget`, `attempt_timeout_seconds`, `max_output_tokens`, `invalid_rate`,
`token_mix`); its candidates and exclusions each carry `canonical`, the model they serve,
beside `model`, the provider's own name. simpl_core records it with the attempt trail as
the call's routing decision.

Every entry of the `attempt_log` carries `attempt_id`, a uuid4 string minted as its
request starts. The request (a stream from its open to its close) runs inside
simpl_tracker's `llm_accounting.attempt_scope` with that id and the upstream the member
is pinned to (`None` when unpinned), so the cost receipt of the request carries
`routing_attempt_id` and `upstream_tag`: simpl_core stores the id as the attempt's
`attempt_key`, and simpl_tracker prices the receipt at the upstream's rate. A
simpl_tracker without `attempt_scope` records its receipts as before. Without an
`attempt_log` there is no entry and no scope. Every entry also carries `family` (the
call's prompt family key, `null` without an agent name) and `prefix_fingerprint`: hashes
of the prefix the request sent (its tool definitions, then its instructions and system
prompt), cumulative at each 4,000-character block (about 1,000 tokens), at most 8 of 16
hex characters, empty for a prefix under one block
(`simpl_ia.agents.affinity.prefix_fingerprint`); two requests share their leading hashes
exactly as far as their leading blocks match. An answer from Cheaper Inference carries
`cache_affinity`, the gateway's `x-ci-prompt-cache-affinity` (`hit`, `miss`, `new`,
`stale`). Every entry carries `capabilities`, the sorted capabilities the member needed
for the request (`simpl_ia.agents.structured.needed_capabilities(target, output_mode=,
tools_present=, reasoning=None)`): `tools` for function tools or tool output,
`structured_output.native`, `.tool` or `.prompted` for the mode a structured answer
resolves to (none for free text), `reasoning` when the target sets a reasoning level. An
accepted answer is evidence the upstream that served it has them; a `capability` error,
that it lacks one. `CompletionClient` entries carry `capabilities: []`. In a call a
running canary drew (below), every entry of that target's members carries `canary_trial`,
the canary's trial id; no other entry has the key.

Every closed entry also carries `health_class`, what the attempt says of its offering
(`simpl_ia.agents.health.classify_attempt(entry)`, a pure function you can run over a
stored trail): `answered` (a decision model's hand-off included); `invalid` (rejected as
`InvalidOutput`, cut off, truncated or past its reasoning budget included, handed back for
a corrective retry, or a reply the client could not read); `timeout` (the attempt
deadline, the first-token deadline, an HTTP client timeout, `TimeoutError`, HTTP 408);
`server` (HTTP 5xx, 429 and any other status not listed, connection errors, any other
error); `capability` (HTTP 400, 404, 422 that is not `context`); `context` (HTTP 413, or
HTTP 400 or 422 whose message names the context window: `maximum context length`,
`context_length_exceeded`, `context window`, `prompt is too long`, `too many tokens`,
`reduce the length`, matched in any case; and `ContextWindowExceeded`: the request is too
long for the model, which says nothing of the offering, so simpl_core's sink counts it
toward no health and marks no capability); `cancelled`; `null` for a refusal that sent nothing (`TargetTimeoutSkip`, an adopted
pause, `AttemptBudgetSpent`). A raised entry carries `http_status` when its error names
one and `timed_out: true` for a timeout. Install a `HealthSink`
(`simpl_ia.agents.health.install_health_sink`; simpl_core installs one that writes the
routing tables) and every classified attempt of every chain built with an `attempt_log`
is handed to its `record(outcome, settings)`: a `HealthOutcome(provider, api, model,
upstream, agent, health_class, latency_ms, at)` (`upstream` and `agent` are `""` when
there is none; `at` is when the attempt closed, seconds since the epoch) and the member's
`HealthSettings`. A failure is handed over at once, an answer once the event-loop step
that returned it is done (the chain may still reject it in that step). The sink is
called synchronously and must never block or raise. `CompletionClient` entries carry
`health_class` too but are not reported.

### Plain completions on a self-hosted model

`CompletionClient` makes one OpenAI-compatible `/chat/completions` call to a
self-hosted server and returns the answer as the server sent it: no agent, no output
tool, no `response_format`. It is for a model whose task travels as request fields
(NuExtract reads its template from `chat_template_kwargs`); `simpl_core`'s `lm_model`
agent kind calls it. It is a separate client, not a mode of `StructuredLLMClient`.

```python
from simpl_ia.agents import (
    FAILURE_UNREACHABLE,
    CompletionClient,
    CompletionFailed,
    ModelPolicy,
)

policy = ModelPolicy(
    name="nuextract3",
    provider="self_hosted",
    base_url="http://127.0.0.1:8092/v1",
    http_timeout_seconds=300,
    extra_body={"chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": False}},
)
client = CompletionClient(policy)  # ValueError unless every target is self_hosted
attempts: list[dict] = []  # optional: filled as requests go, kept if the call is cancelled
try:
    completion = await client.complete(
        document,
        max_tokens=1024,  # temperature defaults to 0
        extra_body={"chat_template_kwargs": {"template": template, "instructions": text}},
        attempts=attempts,
    )
except CompletionFailed as failed:
    failed.attempts  # every request of the call, each with outcome "raised" and a failure
    server_down = all(a["failure"] == FAILURE_UNREACHABLE for a in failed.attempts)
else:
    completion.text, completion.finish_reason  # "" / None when the server sent none
    completion.target  # SelectedTargetMetadata of the member that answered
    completion.attempts  # every request, the failed ones before the answer included
```

- **The request**: the prompt is the only message, a user message; `max_tokens`,
  `temperature` and the call's `extra_body` merged over the target's (nested objects
  merge, the call wins). The key is the target's bound key, else `EMPTY`. An
  `extra_body` setting `model`, `messages`, `max_tokens`, `temperature` or `stream` is
  refused with `ValueError`. A target with `reasoning` or a non-`auto` `output_mode` is
  refused when the client is built.
- **Fallback and retries**: targets are asked in policy order. A connection that fails
  or a busy status (408, 409, 429, 502, 503, 504; `RETRY_STATUSES`) is asked again on the
  same target, up to `max_attempts_per_target` (3) requests, pausing
  `retry_pause_seconds` (2 s) then twice that. Any other error status (500 included), a
  dropped connection, an attempt timeout, or a body with no choice moves to the next
  target at once. At most `len(policy.targets()) * max_attempts_per_target` requests per
  call.
- **Attempt timeout**: wall clock per request, the target's `http_timeout_seconds`, else
  the environment's default (60 s, `AI_LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`). Set it on a slow model.
  Connecting gets at most 10 s (`CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`), half the attempt timeout
  when that is shorter, so a server that never accepts fails as `unreachable`.
- **No checks on the answer**: a cut answer (`finish_reason="length"`) or an empty one is
  returned; the caller decides.
- **Attempt entries** carry the keys of `agents/attempts.py` (`outcome`, `model`,
  `provider`, `fallback_index`, `latency_ms`, `finish_reason`, `error_class`,
  `error_message`, ...) plus `failure` on a raised one, exported as `FAILURE_*`:
  `unreachable` (no connection), `dropped` (the server took the request and closed the
  connection without an answer), `http_status` (with `http_status`), `timeout` or
  `malformed`. Pass `attempts=` to receive them as they are sent: a call cancelled by
  the caller's deadline leaves its request in flight there with outcome `cancelled`.
- **HTTP client**: requests go through the event loop's cached client, the one
  `StructuredLLMClient` uses. A `transport=` is wrapped in a client of its own and never
  closed: the caller owns it.
- **No cost receipt**: no provider bills a self-hosted call.

### Decision models (`api="decisions"`)

A decision model is not a language model: it answers the output type's fields
as typed questions about the user prompt (a yes/no with its probability, a pick
from a `Literal`/`Enum`, a rubric level) in a few hundred milliseconds, and
generates no text. Use one for routing, triage, classification, gating and
moderation -- one-second human judgements -- with a language model behind it
for whatever it hands off. Every decision model on OpenRouter (TypeSafe's Jev,
Perplexity's Decider, any other) is reached the same way: name it like a chat
model on OpenRouter and add `api="decisions"`. There is no list of decision
models; any id works, and one OpenRouter does not serve is its 400, as for a
chat model. Full detail in `docs/provider_routing.md`, "Decision models".

```python
from simpl_ia.agents import DecisionThresholds, ModelPolicy, ModelTarget, OpenRouterRouting


class Triage(BaseModel):
    """Route a support message."""  # the docstring is the goal routes are weighed by

    urgent: bool = Field(description="Does this need a reply within the hour?")
    team: Literal["billing", "technical", "other"] = Field(
        description="Which team should handle this?"
    )


policy = ModelPolicy(
    name="perplexity/pplx-decider-v1.1-27b-20261006",  # or "typesafe/jev-1.13-20260917", ...
    provider="openrouter",
    api="decisions",
    provider_routing=OpenRouterRouting(zdr=True),  # sent as the request's `provider`
    decision_thresholds=DecisionThresholds(boolean=0.7),  # what `True` has to mean
    fallbacks=[ModelTarget(name="z-ai/glm-5.3-flash", provider="openrouter")],
)
# The same `openrouter` binding keys both members.
bindings = [
    ProviderBinding(
        provider="openrouter",
        credentials=ProviderCredentials(api_key=os.environ["OPENROUTER_API_KEY"]),
    ),
]
client = StructuredLLMClient.from_policy(bind_policy_credentials(policy, bindings))
agent = client.make_agent(output_type=Triage, system_prompt="<context to judge>")
result = await client.run(agent=agent, user_prompt="I was charged twice.")
result.output                                        # Triage(urgent=True, team="billing")
result.response.provider_details["confidence"]       # {"urgent": 0.7, "team": 0.8}
client.selected_target_metadata(result).api          # "decisions"
```

`provider="typesafe"` is an alias: a target naming it is
`provider="openrouter", api="decisions"` from the moment it is validated, so
deployed policies (`typesafe/jev-1.13-20260917`, `jev-1.13`, `jev-latest`) keep
loading and answering on this route, keyed by the `openrouter` binding. A host
that still binds `typesafe` explicitly has that binding honoured for decision
targets.

Rules that follow from what a decision model is:

- **The question lives on the output type**, in `Field(description=...)`, the
  model's docstring and `client.run(..., instructions=...)`. The **system prompt
  is material the model judges** (sent as history beside the user prompt), so
  keep it to context and keep the state small: irrelevant detail is a distractor.
- **Every field must be a question the model can answer**: `bool`,
  `Literal`/`Enum` of strings, `float` with `ge=0, le=1`, `list` of a
  `Literal`/`Enum`, an `IntEnum` rubric described per level,
  `Literal[...] | None`, or a nested model of these. A `str`, an unbounded number
  or a date is refused with `UserError` before any request -- no fallback runs,
  it is a wrong output type. Extract free text with a language model, and let the
  decision model decide about it. Each model's API enforces its own limits on
  options and levels.
- **Do not ask it to compute**: decision models do not count, compare dates or do
  arithmetic reliably. Do that in code and ask about the result. Add an `other` /
  `None` option where a taxonomy may not cover every input, and test option
  orderings -- reordering `Literal` members can shift answers.
- **Thresholds are policy**: `decision_thresholds.boolean` (default 0.5) and
  `.route` (unset: the likeliest pick of a tool or output is taken; set, a pick
  less likely than it is handed to the next member). Tune them on labelled
  examples against a dated snapshot, the only name that cannot change model
  under you.
- `reasoning`, `output_mode="native"` and `output_mode="prompted"` are refused on
  a decision target; `temperature` is not sent; `run_stream` yields the whole
  answer as one event.
- In a chain, a step the model hands off (`UnfillableRoute`: a tool or output it
  cannot fill; `UnsureRoute`: a pick below `route`) and any API failure advance
  to the next member; a language-model primary failing hands the step to a
  decision-model fallback. Only the hand-offs cost a language-model call. **A
  decision model last or alone with tools** raises `UnfillableRoute` to the
  caller whenever it prefers a tool it cannot fill: put a language model behind
  it. Decision and chat members mix freely; one name may appear once per API.
- Transient failures (408, 429, 5xx, a dropped connection) are retried twice,
  within 2 s when a member follows and within 30 s when none does. A turn can be
  two requests; a second that still fails hands the whole step to the member
  behind, which answers it from the start.
- Answers report a dated snapshot of the model asked for, or of what a `-latest`
  alias resolved to. A chain with **a `-latest` alias beside the release it
  resolves to** must pass `attempt_log` to `make_agent`: by name the two are
  indistinguishable.
- Cost is recorded under the **`openrouter`** provider at the `usage.cost`
  OpenRouter reports, with its generation id and the upstream, so decision calls
  reconcile against the OpenRouter export like any other. An answer that reports
  no cost falls back to `simpl_tracker`'s rate rows.

### Streaming a turn

`client.run_stream(agent=..., user_prompt=...)` returns pydantic-ai's own
streaming handle — an async context manager whose iterator yields the run's
events and ends with the result event:

```python
async with client.run_stream(agent=agent, user_prompt="...") as events:
    async for event in events:
        ...
```

It differs from `run` in what it refuses to do, not in what it resolves:

- **Output retries are pinned to zero** (`retries={"output": 0}`): the text has
  already been shown, so a schema-invalid payload fails the turn instead of
  re-asking the model. Tool retries are untouched. An agent whose `ToolOutput`
  carries its own `max_retries` is refused with `ValueError` — build agents with
  `make_agent`.
- **None of `run`'s post-hoc resilience applies**: no truncate-on-context-window
  retry, no temperature retry. Pass `model_settings` only for a setting every
  chain member accepts, or leave it unset.
- **Per-segment enforcement**: `reject_unfinished_segments=True` wraps the agent's
  model in `SegmentCompletionGuard`, which ends the run the moment a response
  segment closes without finishing (error, filtered, incomplete) — *before* the
  SDK runs the tools that segment asked for. Pass the same `attempt_log` you gave
  `make_agent` so the refused segment's trail entry is corrected.
- **The per-attempt bound covers the open only.** A target's resolved deadline (its
  `attempt_timeout_seconds`, the policy's, then `make_agent(...,
  attempt_timeout_seconds=)`) wraps each of its members in `AttemptDeadline`; on a
  stream it bounds opening the stream, so a target that never starts raises and
  the chain advances. Once chunks flow, that clock is off. A target that goes
  quiet mid-stream is ended by the HTTP per-read timeout or by *your* ceiling over
  the whole turn — nothing infers a stall from silence.
- **A target that keeps timing out is skipped.** A chain member stops sending to a
  target that reached its threshold of timeouts (default 5) in the window (default
  300 s) and timed out more often than it responded. The member raises
  `TargetTimeoutSkip` before sending, so the chain moves on with no wait and nothing
  is billed; the trail shows `raised` with that `error_class`. After the cooldown
  (default 300 s, for every member) one call at a time tries it again. A chain's last
  member never refuses. Counts live in the process, keyed by provider, API, model,
  pinned upstream and agent (`make_agent(agent_name=...)`), so every policy of the same
  agent naming the same model and upstream adds to one count; chains built without an
  agent name share one count; a short-lived run starts with none.
- **Each policy sets its own skip values.** `timeout_skip: {threshold, window_seconds,
  cooldown_seconds}` on a target, else on the policy (the primary's), else the host's
  `AI_TIMEOUT_SKIP_*` variables, else the defaults in `simpl_ia.agents.structured`. A
  threshold of `0` turns the skip off for that target. A skip stays in the process that
  started it; its timeouts reach other processes as health outcomes (below).
- **`routing: internal` lets simpl_ia pick an OpenRouter target's upstream.** Each
  request is pinned to one upstream (`only: [tag]`, the target's other routing fields
  kept). A request that fails with an error the chain falls back on (a provider
  error, or a body that is not a usable completion such as invalid JSON), or whose
  response the chain rejects (cut off before answering, or invalid with no retry
  left), moves to the next upstream while the model's attempt budget (above) lasts,
  then the chain moves to its next member. Every attempt keeps the
  target's `fallback_index` and carries the pinned tag as `upstream` in the trail.
  The candidates come from the process's offering source
  (`simpl_ia.agents.routing_table.install_offering_source`; simpl_core installs its
  routing table there): every offering of the model that passes the target's `zdr`,
  `quantizations`, `only`, `ignore`, closed `order` and `max_price` (prompt and
  completion), and that accepts the parameters the request needs, when the offering
  lists its parameters: `tools` for function tools or tool output, `reasoning`, and
  `response_format` when the request resolves to native structured output, as
  `output_mode: auto` does on most OpenRouter models. The candidates are **ranked by
  estimated cost per call**: prompt price × the agent's input tokens + completion price
  × its output tokens, from the source's `token_mix(agent, provider, model)` (a
  `TokenMix(input_tokens, output_tokens, samples)`), or 3 input tokens per output token
  when it has none; unpriced offerings come after priced ones, and degraded ones (the
  gateway's negative `status`, or `health: degraded`) after everything in service. A closed list (`only`, or `order` with `allow_fallbacks:
  false`) is the candidate set, ranked, not walked in its written order; an open
  `order` keeps the upstreams it names first, ranked among themselves, then ranks the
  rest. The written order only breaks ties. `sort: price` is this order, and `sort:
  latency` or `throughput` is refused on an internal target. Each `Offering` carries
  `prompt_price`, `completion_price` and `cache_read_price` in USD per token,
  `price_source` (`book` for the price book, `listing` for the gateway's listing) and
  `cancel_stops_billing` (`true` when a cancelled stream there is billed no more than it
  streamed, `null` unknown; only marked offerings are hedged, above).
  `routing_table.rank_offerings` gives the ranking and the offerings left out with
  their reason; `candidate_upstreams` the upstream tags alone.
  `data_collection` and the other `max_price` limits are left to OpenRouter, which
  applies them to the pinned request. A caller that bounds the whole call takes its
  ceiling from `chain_call_ceiling_seconds(targets, attempt_timeout_seconds=...,
  retries=3)` (above), the sum of every model's time share; `chain_attempt_budgets(targets,
  retries=3)` still gives the sum of every model's attempt budget. Without a source, or for a model it does not list, the
  target's own closed list (`only`, or `order` with `allow_fallbacks: false`) is used
  in its written order; with neither, the target goes to OpenRouter as written and a
  warning is logged.
  The default, `routing: gateway` (or unset), lets OpenRouter pick, with one
  exception: while the timeout skip is on, a closed list is sent one upstream at a
  time in the same way, so a stalled upstream is skipped alone; those skips stay in
  the process. With the skip off, `provider_routing` goes as written.
- **An internal target can rank its upstreams by effective cost and speed.** The same
  candidates are also scored (`simpl_ia.agents.scoring`, pure) from what the source
  measured: `offering_stats(agent, provider, model, upstream)` (an `OfferingStats(samples,
  attempts, p_fail, p_late, latency_p50_ms, latency_p90_ms, latency_p95_ms, ttft_p50_ms,
  tokens_per_s, cache_hit_share, answered_cost_usd, failed_cost_usd, ttft_p95_ms,
  by_input_size)`, the agent's figures or the offering's pooled ones) and
  `token_profile(agent, provider, model)` (a `TokenProfile(samples, input_p50, input_p90,
  cached_p50, output_p50, output_p90, reasoning_p50, reasoning_p90)`); a source without
  them measures nothing. `by_input_size` maps an input-size bucket (`lt_2k` below 2,000
  input tokens, `2k_8k`, `8k_32k`, `ge_32k` from 32,000; `routing_table.INPUT_SIZES`,
  `routing_table.input_size(tokens)`) to an `OfferingStats` of the calls of that size: the
  latencies, times to first chunk and tokens per second of the call's bucket replace the
  overall ones, each one the bucket leaves `None` falling back to the overall figure
  (`scoring.for_input_size`). The call's bucket comes from the profile's median input,
  else `make_agent(prompt_chars=)` (the rendered prompt's length, system and user parts)
  / 4; with neither, the overall figures (`scoring.call_input_size`). With Î the
  profile's median input (else the token mix's, else 3 × Ô), Ĥ = Î × `cache_hit_share`
  and Ô its median output plus reasoning (else the mix's, else 1): cost per call C =
  input price × (Î − Ĥ) + cache-read price (else the input price) × Ĥ + output price × Ô;
  P(bad) = p_fail + (1 − p_fail) × p_late; cost per usable answer K = (C + (1 − p̂) ×
  `failed_cost_usd`) / p̂ with p̂ = max(1 − P(bad), 0.01); latency T = TTFT + Ô /
  tokens per second, else the median latency. `objective: {minimize, tie_band, then,
  latency_value_usd_per_s}` (`simpl_ia.agents.ObjectiveConfig`) orders them: `cost` (the
  default) the lowest K and, within `tie_band` (0.10) of it, the best on `then` (`tps`,
  the default, or `latency`); `latency` the lowest T; `blend` the lowest K + λ × T (λ =
  `latency_value_usd_per_s`, required). `constraints: {min_tps, max_ttft_s,
  max_p90_latency_s, max_input_price_per_mtok, max_output_price_per_mtok,
  max_cost_per_call_usd, max_p_bad}` (`ConstraintsConfig`) leave upstreams out with
  `tps_below_min`, `ttft_above_max`, `p90_latency_over_max`, `price_above_max`,
  `cost_per_call_above_max`, `p_bad_above_max`; an unknown figure never excludes, and
  when they leave out every upstream they are kept only as `shadow`. An upstream without
  statistics is scored on its price with no failures, after an equal one with them, so a
  source that measures nothing ranks by price. Degraded upstreams, and those an open
  `order` does not name, still rank after the rest. `ranking: price | effective`
  (default `price`) picks what is sent: `price` sends the price order above; `effective`
  sends the effective order, moves a uniformly drawn upstream of the best's band whose
  P(bad) is at most 1 point above the best's to the front `exploration_share` of the time
  (default 0.04; 0 off; the process's draws come from
  `scoring.install_exploration_random(random.Random(seed))`), and sends each upstream
  with an `adaptive_deadline: {enabled, factor, floor_seconds, min_samples}`
  (`AdaptiveDeadlineConfig`: clamp(1.5 × its p95 latency, 20 s, the target's deadline)
  once its statistics rest on 30 answered attempts; an unbounded target stays
  unbounded). The effective ranking is computed and logged in both modes. Each value is
  the target's, else the policy's (the primary's), field by field for the nested ones,
  else the default (`structured.ranking_settings`). Model order never changes: only one
  target's upstreams are reordered.
- **An internal target routes by each offering's validated capabilities**, in both
  ranking modes. Each `Offering` carries `capabilities`, a mapping of capability
  (`tools`, `structured_output.native`, `structured_output.tool`,
  `structured_output.prompted`, `reasoning`, `caching`, `context`;
  `routing_table.CAPABILITIES`) to status (`certified`, `observed`, `unknown`, `failed`);
  a capability it does not name, or `capabilities=None`, is `unknown`. For the call's
  needed capabilities (`needed_capabilities`, above): an offering `failed` for one is left
  out (`not_capable:<capability>`, the first in sorted order); within its band, the
  offerings `certified` or `observed` for every one rank before those with any `unknown`,
  whatever their cost or score, and exploration stays among the best's equals.
  `certification_required: true` on a target, else on the policy (default false;
  `structured.certification_required_for`) also leaves out an offering with an `unknown`
  one (`not_certified:<capability>`). When this would leave out every offering the routing
  filters kept, all of them are sent (unknown ones before failed ones) and the exclusions
  are logged with `shadow: true`, so a model is never skipped for missing marks.
  `rank_offerings(..., needed_capabilities=, certification_required=)` gives the same
  ranking; each `RankedOffering` carries its `capability_tier` (`0` validated, `1` with an
  unknown, `2` with a failure) and each `ExcludedOffering` its `shadow`.
- **An internal or canonical target leaves out the precisions the agent may not be served
  at**, in both ranking modes. The source's `precision_policy(agent, model)` (read by
  `getattr`; `model` is the provider's own name on a target naming a provider, the
  canonical name on a canonical one) returns a
  `simpl_ia.agents.routing_table.PrecisionPolicy(never, excluded, allowed, ladder,
  default_min, unknown)`: `ladder` the catalog's rungs from the highest precision down,
  `default_min` the lowest rung every agent may be served at (`None`: no floor),
  `allowed` and `excluded` the precisions cleared and excluded for this agent, `never`
  the precisions the catalog allows for no agent, and `unknown` (`allowed` or
  `requires_clearance`) the rule for a precision no rung names, `unknown` included;
  names as offerings report them (`fp8`, `fp4`, `unknown`, ...), compared without case.
  `never` and `excluded` exclude whatever the rung; a precision on a rung below
  `default_min`'s is excluded unless `allowed` names it; one no rung names stays with
  `unknown: allowed`. An offering at an excluded precision (`unknown` when it reports
  none) is left out with `precision_excluded:<precision>`, after the routing filters and
  before the capabilities; when that would leave out every offering the routing filters
  kept (across every provider of a canonical target), all are sent and the exclusions are
  logged with `shadow: true`. Without a policy every precision routes, `unknown` included.
  `rank_offerings(..., precision_policy=)` and `canonical.rank_across_providers(...,
  precision_policy=)` give the same ranking.
- **An agent blamed for its failures on an offering tries it last**, in both ranking
  modes, on internal, canonical and bucket targets. `Offering.blamed_agents` names the
  agents whose own load the offering's failures are blamed on (failing there while the
  other agents it serves do not); the source fills it. For such an agent the offering
  ranks after every other candidate, whatever its precision rank, band, capability tier,
  cost or score and under every objective, and the rest keep their order
  (`RankedOffering.blamed`, `ScoredOffering.blamed`, `routing_table.blamed_for`).
  Exploration never draws it while the best is not blamed. An epoch home it is blamed on
  goes last in its call (`override:blamed`); a planned epoch draws its home as if no
  upstream were blamed, so one agent's blame never moves its family's home. It is never
  left out: the route log lists it in `excluded` with `blamed` and `shadow: true`. Every
  other agent ranks it as usual. `rank_offerings(..., agent_name=)` and
  `canonical.rank_across_providers(..., agent_name=)` give the same ranking. A running
  canary's draw still puts its offering first on its share of calls.
- **A running canary sends a share of the agent's calls to its offering first**, in both
  ranking modes. The source's `canary(agent, model)` (read by `getattr`, `model` as above)
  returns a `simpl_ia.agents.routing_table.CanaryPlan(trial_id, upstream, provider,
  share)`: `upstream` the offering's tag (`""` for a provider without sub-providers),
  `provider` `None` or the target's provider on a typed target, the member's provider on a
  canonical one (`None` there only when a single member has that upstream). Each call
  whose upstreams include the offering draws from the process's canary random
  (`simpl_ia.agents.canary.install_canary_random(random.Random(seed))`, apart from the
  exploration random): with probability `share` (clamped to [0, 1]) the offering goes
  first and the rest keep the order the mode sent, exploration and epoch included. A call
  not drawn is a control: the offering never goes first there; when it would have, it
  goes right behind the best other upstream. The route log's `canary` records `trial`,
  `chosen`, `propensity` (`share` when chosen, `1 − share` when not, `1` when the
  offering was not among the upstreams sent, which draws nothing) and `control` (true on
  a call not drawn whose first upstream is another offering; false when chosen, not
  drawn, or when the offering is the target's only upstream, so no control was possible). A closed list taken as written and a gateway target read neither the
  precision policy nor the canary.
- **A target can name a canonical model instead of a provider.** `ModelTarget(name=
  "deepseek-v4.1-flash")` (no `provider`; also a policy's primary or any fallback) names a
  model of the model catalog the offering source holds: `catalog_model(name)` returns a
  `simpl_ia.agents.routing_table.CatalogModel(name, offers, attempt_timeout_seconds,
  max_output_tokens, max_invalid_responses, zdr)`, `offers` mapping each provider to its
  own name for the model (`{"deepseek": "deepseek-flash", "openrouter":
  "deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash"}`), read by `getattr`. When the agent is built the target
  becomes one chain position with a member on each offered provider whose credentials the
  policy was bound with (`bind_policy_credentials` keeps the whole binding on the policy as
  `provider_credentials`, and leaves such a target unbound): one on DeepSeek, OpenAI or
  Cheaper Inference, each with that provider's route, name and settings, and one per
  OpenRouter endpoint the source lists (pinned as on internal routing; with none listed,
  one that OpenRouter routes). They are ranked together, by price or, with `ranking:
  effective`, by the scoring above, at the token mix and profile with the most samples
  among the providers' models; a provider without sub-providers is read as its offering
  with upstream `""` (`offerings(provider, model)`, `offering_stats(agent, provider, model,
  "")`), and is unpriced when none is listed. They share one attempt budget: the target's
  `max_invalid_responses`, else the catalog model's, else the policy's, else `retries +
  1`; the catalog model's `attempt_timeout_seconds` and `max_output_tokens` likewise stand
  between the target's own and the policy's (`chain_call_ceiling_seconds` reads them from
  the catalog). `require_zdr: true` (only on such a target, not inherited) keeps only
  zero-data-retention members: an OpenRouter endpoint by its listing, a provider without
  sub-providers by the catalog's `zdr` map, else its listing. Left out, with `upstream`
  `""` in the route log: a provider without credentials (`no_credentials`), one that
  refuses the target's `output_mode` or `reasoning` (`incompatible:output_mode`,
  `incompatible:reasoning`), and one no member can be built on (`unsupported_provider`).
  A member whose `Offering.health` is `open` is left out with `health_open` (first-party
  offerings have no pause row, so this is what keeps them unsent; OpenRouter endpoints are
  also paused), unless every member is open: then all stay, logged `shadow: true`;
  `probing` and `degraded` members stay as before. Health is judged before the precision
  policy and capabilities, so when the filters together would leave no member, a healthy
  member they exclude is sent as shadow rather than an open one. A
  name the catalog does not list, or an offer its provider cannot take, raises
  `ValueError` at `make_agent`; a target left with no member refuses every request unsent,
  so the chain moves on. Before the routing table's first load the source may say so with
  an optional `catalog_loaded() -> bool` (read by `getattr`; absent means loaded): while
  it is false, an unlisted name is not an error but a target that refuses every request
  unsent (its route log entry has no candidates), logged once per name as
  `ai.catalog_not_loaded`; once it is true, an unlisted name raises again.
  `provider_routing`, `prompt_cache`, `base_url`, `api_key`,
  `routing` and `api="decisions"` are refused on it. No prompt-family epoch applies to it.
  `resolved_output_modes()` raises for it: each member runs in its own provider's mode. A
  target naming a provider is built as before, whatever the catalog lists.
- **A target can name a bucket instead of a model,** for an agent that names no model.
  `ModelTarget(bucket="fast_check")` / `ModelPolicy(bucket=...)` (no `name`, no `provider`;
  a policy's primary or any fallback) names a bucket the offering source's
  `catalog_bucket(name)` returns (read by `getattr`): a
  `simpl_ia.agents.routing_table.CatalogBucket(name, tiers, objective, constraints,
  require_zdr)`, `tiers` a tuple of tuples of canonical model names (`catalog_model`
  resolves each). When the agent is built each tier becomes one chain position, so a target
  after the bucket has the `fallback_index` of its position (tiers counted), not of its
  place in the policy. A tier's position holds every offering of each of its models, each
  model expanded as a canonical target is (credentials, ZDR, output mode, precision policy
  and canary per model), ranked together: by price, or with `ranking: effective` by the
  scoring above with the bucket's `objective` and `constraints` between the target's own and
  the policy's, each offering scored at its own model's token profile, and its cost per
  usable answer divided by 1 − the agent's invalid-answer rate on its model, from the
  source's `model_fit(agent, model)` (a float in [0, 1] read by `getattr`; `None` for no
  penalty). Each model keeps its own attempt budget, time share, deadline and cap: the catalog model's
  `max_invalid_responses`, else the target's, else the policy's, else `retries + 1` (and
  likewise for `attempt_timeout_seconds` and `max_output_tokens`); a tier is passed over
  once every model's budget is spent or every member failed, so the chain moves to the next
  tier, and later tiers serve only then. `require_zdr` on the bucket, or on the target, keeps
  only zero-data-retention offerings. An open offering is left out of its tier
  (`health_open`), unless every offering of the tier is open (then `shadow`). A bucket or a
  tier model the catalog does not list raises `ValueError` at `make_agent`, as does
  `make_agent(model=...)` with a bucket primary; while the source's `catalog_loaded()` is
  false, an unlisted bucket instead stands at one position (route log `bucket` set, `tier`
  `null`) and a tier with an unlisted model at its own, each refusing unsent and logged
  once. A tier with no member (no credentials for any of its models' providers) refuses
  unsent, so the chain moves on. `provider_routing`, `prompt_cache`, `base_url`, `api_key`,
  `routing`, `decision_thresholds` and `api="decisions"` are refused on it, and
  `resolved_output_modes()` raises for it. `chain_call_ceiling_seconds` and
  `chain_attempt_budgets` count every model of every tier.
- **An internal target's upstreams can be paused for one agent or for every agent,
  from the health outcomes every process reports** (above). The `HealthSettings` each
  outcome carries are the rule the sink applies, set per policy as `health: {...}`
  (`simpl_ia.agents.HealthConfig`) on a target, else on the policy (the primary's), else the defaults in
  `simpl_ia.agents.health.HealthSettings` (no environment variable): over
  `window_seconds` (600), once `min_attempts` (20) remain, an offering's rate of timeouts
  and server errors degrades it at `degrade_rate` (0.10), opens it (a pause for every
  agent) at `open_rate` (0.25), and a degraded one recovers below `recover_rate`
  (0.05). An open offering stays paused `first_cooldown_seconds` (300), doubled at each
  reopening up to `max_cooldown_seconds` (86400); then it is probing, and
  `probe_answers` (3) answers make it healthy. An agent failing at `open_rate` on an
  offering whose other agents have `misfit_min_other_attempts` (10) or more attempts
  there and fail below `degrade_rate` does not count toward that offering, one-offering
  models included; an agent left out on `misfit_min_offerings` (2) or more offerings is a
  workload misfit. No offering opens while `fleet_guard_share` (0.5) of the offerings
  with attempts are degraded or open. Two or more capability errors of one agent pause
  the offering for that agent for `capability_cooldown_seconds` (86400). `enabled:
  false` reports nothing. Each `Offering` carries its `health` (`healthy`, `degraded`,
  `open`, `probing` or `None`) and its pauses as `pauses: tuple[OfferingPause(agent,
  paused_at, paused_until, ended_at), ...]` (`agent=None` for every agent; seconds
  since the epoch). Each call adopts the offering's pauses for every agent and for its
  own agent: the upstream is skipped until `paused_until`, then each process's next
  call is its trial. A trial changes only its own process; a pause ends when it runs
  out or by hand (`ended_at`), which also ends this process's skip that started before
  it, unless its trial is running.
- **A prompt family stays on a warm cache.** Pass `make_agent(agent_name=..., prompt_id=...)`:
  the agent, its prompt id and its tool names make the call's family
  (`simpl_ia.agents.affinity.family_key`, 16 hex characters; none without `agent_name`).
  Its key is sent where a provider can use it: OpenRouter's body `session_id` (chat, every
  target); OpenAI's `prompt_cache_key` on OpenAI's own endpoint for the models before
  GPT-5.6 (`affinity.OPENAI_PROMPT_CACHE_KEY_FAMILIES`: gpt-4o, gpt-4.1, gpt-5 to
  gpt-5.5, o1, o3, o4-mini, and their `-` variants); Cheaper Inference's
  `x-ci-prompt-cache-session` header, with `x-ci-prompt-cache-scope: org` when the
  source's `family_agents(family)` names more than one agent; nothing to DeepSeek. No
  request ever carries Cheaper Inference's `x-ci-techniques`: one whose settings would is
  refused unsent with `simpl_ia.agents.cheaper_inference.TechniquesRefused`. Within one
  agent run, an internal target's next request starts on the upstream that answered its
  last one, in either ranking mode. In `effective` mode, with a source that has
  `family_epoch(family, provider, model)` (an `EpochChoice(upstream, epoch_start,
  epoch_end, snapshot_version)`, the family's latest, in force or ended;
  `simpl_ia.agents.routing_table`), a call starts on the family's epoch home instead of
  drawing an exploration (`simpl_ia.agents.epochs`): `kept` while the home is a candidate,
  `healthy`, within the target's `max_p90_latency_s` and not left out by a constraint;
  otherwise the best other upstream goes first (`override:<reason>`: `not_candidate`,
  `degraded`, `open`, `probing`, `p90_latency_over_max`, a constraint code, or `blamed`
  when the call's agent is blamed for its failures there) and the epoch goes on. With none in force, the process plans one, `min(1,800 s, the provider's
  cache TTL)` long (`affinity.CACHE_TTL_SECONDS`: OpenAI 5 min before GPT-5.6 and 30 min
  after, Anthropic 5 min, DeepSeek hours, other OpenRouter vendors 10 min, else 5 min), on
  a grid every process computes alike: a Thompson draw (each upstream's chance to fail
  from Beta(1 + p_fail × attempts, 1 + the rest), ranked by the objective at its warm
  cache share, `cache_warmth(family, provider, model, upstream)`) seeded by the family,
  the epoch start and the source's `snapshot_version()` gives the challenger, and the
  previous home is left only when n_f × (K_h − K_c,warm) > 2 × (K_c,cold − K_c,warm) and
  K_c,warm < 0.95 × K_h (n_f = `family_rate(family)`, else 1; K at the warm share and at
  0), or when it is no longer fit (`new` with no previous home, else `switched` or
  `kept`). Each source method is read by `getattr` and must answer from memory. The plan
  goes once to the process's `EpochSink` (`simpl_ia.agents.epochs.install_epoch_sink`;
  `record(EpochPlan(family, provider, model, upstream, epoch_start, epoch_end,
  snapshot_version, candidates, draw, propensity))`, called synchronously, must never
  block or raise), and this process follows it until the source lists an epoch. `price`
  mode never reads epochs.

## Milvus: one hit per group, aliases, raw rows and filters

Both `MilvusStore` and `AsyncMilvusStore` (from `simpl_ia.core.vector_stores.milvus`):

- **Filter dicts** (`filter=`): `{"f": v}` is `==`, `{"f": None}` is `IS NULL`, a list is
  `in`, `{"f": {"gte": 1, "lt": 9}}` a range, `{"f": {"ne": v}}` is `!=`,
  `{"f": {"not_in": [...]}}` is `not in`, `{"f": {"not": <any condition on f>}}` wraps it in
  `not (...)`, `{"f": {"is_null": True}}` / `{"is_not_null": True}` write `IS NULL` /
  `IS NOT NULL`. A null field fails every test, negations included. Range operands are
  finite numbers, datetimes or strings; `array_length` takes ints. A bad filter raises
  `MilvusFilterExpressionError` (a `MilvusError`). Full syntax: the `filter_translator`
  module docstring.
- **`AsyncMilvusStore.query_iterator`** sends template values (`use_filter_templates`) to
  Milvus, raises `MilvusError` when a batch passes `timeout` (no silent partial answer),
  and with `output_fields=["*"]` (the default) and no `with_vectors` drops only the
  schema's vector fields from payloads (long ARRAY fields stay; `scroll` too).

- **One best hit per value of a field**: pass `group_by=GroupBy("company_id")` to
  `search` and `text_search`, or set `group_by` on `HybridSearchConfig` /
  `MilvusMultiBM25HybridSearchConfig`. `limit` / `top_k` then counts groups. Grouping
  runs on the server after the filter, so a filtered search over many companies returns
  one hit for every company that has a match. The iterators refuse it.
- **Long value lists in hybrid filters**: `MilvusMultiBM25HybridSearchConfig(...,
  use_filter_templates=True)` sends the filter as a template with its values as
  parameters; `HybridSearchRequest(filter_expr=..., filter_params=...)` does the same per
  request. A `HybridSearchRequest` with `metric_type="BM25"` takes the query text as its
  `vector`.
- **Aliases**: `create_alias`, `alter_alias` (atomic for readers), `drop_alias`,
  `describe_alias` (target name, or `None`), `list_aliases`. Search, query and count
  accept an alias wherever they take a collection name. A collection with aliases
  cannot be dropped until they are removed.
- **Your own schema**: `create_collection_from_schema(name, schema, index_params,
  num_partitions=, consistency_level=)` for partition keys, nullable fields, scalar
  indexes or a BM25 function with a custom analyzer (build them with
  `MilvusClient.create_schema()` / `prepare_index_params()`).
- **Rows as the schema has them**: `upsert_rows(name, rows, partial_update=False,
  batch_size=500)`. With `partial_update=True` each row carries its key and the fields
  to change; Milvus keeps the rest, vectors and text included. (`upsert` instead adds
  `id` / `vector` itself and its `partial_update` only switches insert to upsert.)
- **Analyzer check**: `run_analyzer(texts, collection_name=, field_name=)` returns the
  tokens BM25 indexes for that field; `run_analyzer(texts, analyzer_params=)` tries an
  analyzer before a collection uses it.

Nebius embeddings: `encoding_format="base64"` sends float32 bytes (half the size of JSON
floats) and callers still get floats. Nebius honours `dimensions` for Qwen3-Embedding.

Nebius rate limits are handled for you. Every Nebius provider in a process shares one
pacer per key and model. It runs at 95% of the account's per-minute limits (read from
each answer's `x-ratelimit-*` headers), makes up pauses within the minute, and steers by
Nebius's own measure of the 15-minute window toward 90%, so Token Factory keeps raising
them. `nebius_pace` lines (every 5 minutes) show where it stands. It waits out a 429 and sends the request again. A 429 raises `ProviderRateLimitError` only if you pass
`max_rate_limit_wait=<seconds>`, e.g. for an interactive search that must answer in
time. `max_retries` covers connection errors, timeouts and 5xx. `provider.pacer.snapshot()`
shows the limits and the pace.

## Chunking along a page's elements

`simpl_ia.core.chunking.blocks` cuts a page given as lines, each with the ids of the
block elements it sits in (outermost first), into chunks that follow the page's
structure. It is plain Python: no chunking extra is needed.

```python
from simpl_ia.core.chunking.blocks import BlockLine, chunk_blocks

lines = [
    BlockLine("# Services", (0, 1)),
    BlockLine("## CNC turning", (0, 2, 3)),
    BlockLine("Parts to drawing, from prototype to series.", (0, 2, 3)),
]
for chunk in chunk_blocks(lines, soft=1200, hard=1800):
    print(chunk.section, chunk.text)   # "Services", the three lines joined by \n
```

- The largest element whose text fits `hard` characters stays in one chunk; an element
  over `hard` is split into its children. A chunk closes at an element boundary once it
  holds `soft` characters and never goes past `hard`.
- Headings are lines starting with `#` marks; `section` is the heading path at the
  chunk's first line, outermost first (`Services > CNC turning`). A heading a chunk
  would end on moves to the next chunk.
- Only a line over `hard` is split, at sentences, then words.
- `chunk.lines` holds the indices of the input lines in the chunk, in order (a split line is
  in each of its chunks), so data kept per line can follow its chunk.

## Rules and conventions

- Prefer `simpl_ia.core` for lightweight imports that must not pull `pymilvus` or torch. Use top-level `simpl_ia` only when Milvus or the full kitchen-sink surface is intentional.
- Declare the correct extra in the consumer's `pyproject.toml` before importing ML, vector-store, chunking, or agents submodules.
- Resolve embedding providers via `ProviderRegistry` entry points (`simpl_ia.providers` in `pyproject.toml`), not hard-coded client construction in app repos.
- Chunkers and refineries register under `simpl_ia.chunkers` / `simpl_ia.refineries` entry points — reuse those names instead of forking adapter code.

## Common pitfalls

- **Don't** `from simpl_ia import MilvusStore` in code paths that must stay pymilvus-free — first access loads pymilvus at import time.
- **Don't** import `simpl_ia.inference.embedders` without the `[ml]` extra — lazy loaders raise `ImportError` with the install hint.
- **Don't** import `simpl_ia.agents` without `[agents]` — it needs `pydantic-ai` and `simpl-tracker`.
- **Don't** add a decision model by name to a list, regex or provider of its own — any model on OpenRouter's decisions API is `provider="openrouter", api="decisions"`.
- **Don't** put the question for a decision-model member in the system prompt — the model judges it as material. Field descriptions, the output type's docstring and `instructions` are what it is asked.
- **Don't** assume `simpl_ia.core.vector_stores.EmbedderMetadata` and `simpl_ia.core.providers.metadata.EmbedderMetadata` are the same class — they are distinct types for different layers.
- **Don't** leave the HTTP timeout at its 60 s default for a call that can stay silent longer — it is dropped and falls back. OpenRouter sends nothing on an unstreamed call until the answer is complete, so a reasoning model there is silent for its whole generation: on 2026-10-03 every 2.5-5 minute call on `deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash` died at 60 s while the upstream finished and billed it. Set `http_timeout_seconds` on the policy target that needs it; `AI_LLM_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`, `AI_SLOW_MODEL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` and `AI_SLOW_MODEL_MARKERS` (see the README) set the default for targets that say nothing.

## Testing

```bash
conda activate simpl_ia
poetry install
poetry run pytest
poetry run ruff check .
```

Mock external embedding/vector APIs at the provider boundary; do not require live Milvus or remote keys in unit tests.

## What this library does NOT do

- Does not own platform business logic or HTTP routes — use `simpl_core` / `simpl_api`.
- Does not own shared ORM models — use `simpl_orm`.
- Does not own cost tracking or Discord notifications — use `simpl_tracker` (though `[agents]` depends on it for tracing).
- Does not price a `cheaper_inference` call at the call: the gateway sends no amount, so the answer carries the gateway's request id (`provider_response_id`) and the amount is matched from its usage export by that id.
- Does not replace Prefect workflow orchestration — use `simpl_flow`.

